Come on, guys, mind flayers aren't all _that_ bad! I've never been
killed by one, and never even been *touched* by one as soon as I
learned to treat them with a little respect. I don't even genocide
them anymore.

Just make sure you have the following:
- intrinsic telepathy and some way to blind yourself. That way you can
follow its progress around the level.
- some fairly effective ranged attack. This can include arrows
(poisoned are great!), daggers (the more enchanted the better), a
nasty attack wand or spell, or numerous other possibilities (i.e.
cockatrice eggs and nasty potions). Mind flayers have none of the
resistances, so you can get them with poison, fire, cold, wand/finger
of death, paralysis, sleep, stoning... the list goes on and on.
- some last-ditch escape method: i.e. wand/spell of teleport/digging
(or scroll of genocide for that matter).
- keep monitoring the beastie as it moves around the level. Pay *close
attention* to your messages: when it "latches on to your telepathy,"
you know it's close!
- try to get it at the end of a long corridor, and keep firing ranged
attacks at it. It's not very fast and if you're expert in a ranged
attack (e.g. Ranger with bow) you should be able to kill it before it
gets even close to you.
- if all else fails, use the method of last resort. Or engrave
Elbereth. (I've never had to do this, though.)
- and just keep your cool, whatever happens!

Like I said, I've never been killed by a (M)MF. Not even close. I
don't know why people make such a big deal about them. My two pet
peeves are soldier ants (way too overpowered for when they show up,
which is early game) and liches (late game) simply because they're a
pain in the rear end. (Oh, and gnomes wielding unexpected wands of
death. :-) ) As long as you follow the cardinal rule of "keep it more
than one square away from you," mind flayers are among the more
harmless denizens of the dungeon. (The one thing I find annoying is
that they can fly, which seems quite arbitrary. I suspect the DevTeam
put it in there just to make them harder to deal with.)

